 ```
 
+### Team communication
+
+Communication is opt-in and available only in parallel mode. With it disabled, subprocess arguments, prompts, tools, and behavior are unchanged. Set `communication: true` and optionally assign stable worker IDs:
+
+```json
+{
+  "tasks": [
+    { "id": "api", "agent": "worker", "task": "Inspect and update the API" },
+    { "id": "tests", "agent": "worker", "task": "Inspect and update the tests" }
+  ],
+  "communication": true
+}
+```
+
+If `id` is omitted, workers are named `worker-1`, `worker-2`, and so on. IDs must be unique within the dispatch and contain 1-64 letters, numbers, underscores, or hyphens.
+
+Communication-enabled workers receive three additional tools:
+
+- `team_list`: Return the worker's own ID and all currently running teammates.
+- `team_send`: Send a private message to one running teammate by ID.
+- `team_broadcast`: Send a message to every other running teammate.
+
+A successful send means the recipient accepted the message for delivery; it does not wait for a reply. Incoming messages are added automatically to the recipient's model context as `Message from teammate "<id>":` followed by the complete message. Pi queues them after the current response and tool calls, so tools are not interrupted. A worker processes accepted messages before it exits.
+
+Messages are limited to 128 KiB of UTF-8 text. Unknown, finished, queued, or self recipients are reported as failures. Broadcast results report every successful and failed recipient. Because parallel execution is limited to four concurrent processes, queued tasks are not addressable until they start.
+
+Each dispatch uses a private authenticated local socket (a Unix-domain socket or Windows named pipe). The socket and credentials are unique to that dispatch and are removed when the dispatch finishes or is cancelled. Separate dispatches cannot exchange messages, even if they reuse worker IDs.
+
